var e=[{to:`/staging-dev-guide/`,label:`Overview`,blurb:`What this guide is and how to use it.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/colors/`,label:`Colors`,blurb:`Semantic colour tokens, gradients and glow utilities.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/typography/`,label:`Typography`,blurb:`The two families and the six-step type scale.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/customers/`,label:`Customers`,blurb:`Every customer and partner logo we ship, by set, with the pages that use it.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/figures/`,label:`Figures & motion`,blurb:`Every figure with its fallback, live on demand; media components and the motion inventory.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/components/`,label:`Components`,blurb:`Primitives with their real props, layout, and the section catalogue.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/rules/`,label:`Rules & build gates`,blurb:`What fails a build, and the conventions behind each gate.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/cards/`,label:`Cards`,blurb:`Live glass, tilt, spotlight, conic-ring, lift and volumetric cards.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/text/`,label:`Text motion`,blurb:`Live split reveals, shimmer, blur-in, count-up and line-draw text.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/buttons/`,label:`Buttons`,blurb:`Live glow, icon-shift, shimmer, trace and loading button primitives.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/`,label:`Images`,blurb:`Every image in public/img with its dimensions, weight and where it is used, plus the frames that present a capture.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/logo-figure/`,label:`Logo figure`,blurb:`Review: the NeuralSeek icon as a point-cloud figure, sway versus spin.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/wire-figures/`,label:`Wire figures`,blurb:`Every turning SVG wire figure, and the customer story each one is on.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/code/`,label:`Code`,blurb:`The code block for NTL snippets: filename bar, copy button, line numbers and brace highlighting.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/sitemap/`,label:`Sitemap`,blurb:`Navbar and footer trees, every site URL with its reachability, and the redirect map.`}],t=[{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/`,label:`Overview`,blurb:`Where images live, how they are encoded, and the redaction rule for captures.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/frames/`,label:`Frames`,blurb:`The live ImageFrame and BrowserFrame treatments: glass, browser chrome, fades, tilt, featured.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/captures/`,label:`Captures`,blurb:`Product console screenshots and the two drawn illustrations, all 1600 wide.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/stills/`,label:`Stills & posters`,blurb:`WebGL fallback stills, portrait crops and the poster frame cut from each video.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/logos/`,label:`Logos`,blurb:`The marquee files and the two NeuralSeek wordmarks as image assets. Every customer and partner logo by set: the Customers page.`},{to:`/staging-dev-guide/images/ring/`,label:`Ring`,blurb:`Several captures on a ring, dragged round instead of scrolled along — no first card, no last.`}],n=[{title:`Surfaces`,description:`The brand surface ramp, darkest chrome to most elevated panel. Every row is a light-dark() pair under the "Theme" heading in styles.css, mapped to utilities in @theme. Left swatch dark, right swatch light; flip the toggle in the sidebar to see the page follow.`,entries:[{name:`--nav-bg`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#0B0A0F`,swatch:`#0B0A0F`,light:`#FFFFFF`,lightSwatch:`#FFFFFF`,note:`Header, footer and the DemoButton face. /85 tint with no blur on the fixed header bar; /70 with backdrop-blur-md for transient glass (dropdown, mobile panel).`,agent:`Use bg-nav-bg (--nav-bg, #0B0A0F) for the header, the footer and glass section bands. The fixed header bar is bg-nav-bg/85 with NO backdrop-blur — it sits over the animating aurora on every page and a backdrop blur there is recomputed every frame. Transient glass (dropdown, mobile panel) is bg-nav-bg/70 backdrop-blur-md. Never hardcode the hex in a component.`},{name:`--canvas`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#131316`,swatch:`#131316`,light:`#FAF9FC`,lightSwatch:`#FAF9FC`,note:`Page background. --background resolves to it in both themes; the light value is the warm-white the whole light palette hangs off.`,agent:`Use bg-background (which resolves to --canvas, #131316) for page backgrounds. Never write bg-black or bg-[#131316].`},{name:`--surface-2`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#1F1C28`,swatch:`#1F1C28`,light:`#F2EFF9`,lightSwatch:`#F2EFF9`,note:`Muted and secondary fills, inactive chips. Also backs --muted, --secondary and --accent.`,agent:`Use bg-surface-2 (--surface-2, #1F1C28) for muted or secondary fills and inactive states.`},{name:`--surface`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#2A2638`,swatch:`#2A2638`,light:`#E9E4F4`,lightSwatch:`#E9E4F4`,note:`Cards (--card) and the base of site-gradient-bg.`,agent:`Use bg-surface (--surface, #2A2638) for card surfaces. It is also the base colour of the site-gradient-bg utility.`},{name:`--surface-elev`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#332F42`,swatch:`#332F42`,light:`#FFFFFF`,lightSwatch:`#FFFFFF`,note:`Popovers (--popover) and raised panels above card level.`,agent:`Use bg-surface-elev (--surface-elev, #332F42) for popovers and raised panels above card level.`},{name:`--wash`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#301E4C`,swatch:`#301E4C`,light:`#DDD3F3`,lightSwatch:`#DDD3F3`,note:`The single brand purple. Washes, glows, gradient stops, the DemoButton inset light leaks.`,agent:`Use --wash (#301E4C, utility bg-wash) as the only brand purple for washes, glows and gradient stops. Never introduce another purple or a generic indigo gradient.`},{name:`--veil`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`oklch(0.05 0.012 300)`,swatch:`oklch(0.05 0.012 300)`,light:`oklch(0.93 0.035 300)`,lightSwatch:`oklch(0.93 0.035 300)`,note:`The DarkBand veil colour — near-black with a hint of the wash hue.`,agent:`Use --veil (oklch(0.05 0.012 300), utility bg-veil) only for the DarkBand scroll veil. It is not a surface colour.`},{name:`--glass`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`oklch(0.12 0.02 275 / 55%)`,swatch:`oklch(0.12 0.02 275 / 55%)`,light:`oklch(0.995 0.005 300 / 70%)`,lightSwatch:`oklch(0.995 0.005 300 / 70%)`,note:`Translucent panel fill for blurred chrome.`,agent:`Use bg-glass (--glass, oklch(0.12 0.02 275 / 55%)) with backdrop-blur for translucent chrome panels, instead of an ad-hoc bg-black/50.`}]},{title:`Lines, glow and text`,entries:[{name:`--hairline`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`oklch(1 0 0 / 12%)`,swatch:`rgba(255,255,255,0.12)`,light:`oklch(0.2 0.03 300 / 12%)`,lightSwatch:`rgba(30,20,50,0.12)`,note:`The only border colour on the site.`,agent:`Use border-hairline (--hairline, oklch(1 0 0 / 12%)) for every border. Never use border-white/10, border-border, border-input or an ad-hoc rgba border.`},{name:`--glow / --glow-soft`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`oklch(0.72 0.15 299) / oklch(0.72 0.15 299 / 40%)`,swatch:`oklch(0.72 0.15 299)`,light:`oklch(0.5 0.19 299) / oklch(0.5 0.19 299 / 40%)`,lightSwatch:`oklch(0.5 0.19 299)`,note:`Accent hue for bloom and shadows. shadow-glow is the pre-built utility (0 0 32px -8px var(--glow-soft)).`,agent:`Use --glow (oklch(0.72 0.15 299)) and --glow-soft for bloom and accent shadows; shadow-glow is the pre-built utility. Do not write a box-shadow with a literal purple.`},{name:`--foreground`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`oklch(0.984 0.003 247.858)`,swatch:`oklch(0.984 0.003 247.858)`,light:`oklch(0.2 0.03 295)`,lightSwatch:`oklch(0.2 0.03 295)`,note:`Primary text.`,agent:`Use text-foreground for primary text. Never use text-white or text-black anywhere in this project.`},{name:`--muted-foreground`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`oklch(0.704 0.04 256.788)`,swatch:`oklch(0.704 0.04 256.788)`,light:`oklch(0.46 0.04 295)`,lightSwatch:`oklch(0.46 0.04 295)`,note:`Body copy, nav links, secondary text. Do not dim it further with an opacity modifier — the axe suite checks contrast on every page.`,agent:`Use text-muted-foreground for body copy, nav links and secondary text. Do not add an opacity modifier like text-muted-foreground/70 — it fails WCAG contrast on the canvas.`}]},{title:`Effect tokens and the theme`,description:`Every gradient stop, trace head, shadow ink and GL uniform on the site comes from these. Highlights invert between themes: the brightest stop on dark is the deepest on light, so an effect still reads as lit against its ground. Defined under the "Theme" heading in styles.css.`,entries:[{name:`Theme model`,path:`src/styles.css · src/lib/theme.ts · src/components/layout/ThemeToggle.tsx`,value:`color-scheme + light-dark() · html[data-theme] override`,note:`First paint follows the OS (prefers-color-scheme) in pure CSS — no theme class in the served HTML. <ThemeToggle> sets data-theme on <html> and mirrors it to one sessionStorage key, ns-theme (allow-listed in RULES.md B1); THEME_BOOT in __root re-applies it before paint on the next page. Per tab: a new tab is back to the OS.`,agent:`The site has two themes resolved by CSS: tokens are light-dark(<light>, <dark>) pairs under the Theme heading in src/styles.css, color-scheme follows the OS, and html[data-theme="light"|"dark"] is the session override set by <ThemeToggle> (src/components/layout/ThemeToggle.tsx). The choice is stored in exactly one sessionStorage key, ns-theme (allow-listed in RULES.md B1 next to the router's scroll store), and THEME_BOOT from src/lib/theme.ts is the only inline script in __root; never add a second storage key, localStorage, a cookie, or a theme class to the served HTML. JS that needs the live theme uses useTheme() (src/hooks/use-theme.ts); JS that needs a colour calls readThemeTokens() from src/lib/theme.ts inside an effect keyed on useTheme().resolved.`},{name:`--brand-hi / --brand / --brand-mid / --brand-deep / --brand-shade`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#C9B8F0 / #A78BFA / #8B6FD1 / #6B4FA8 / #301E4C`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #C9B8F0, #A78BFA, #8B6FD1, #6B4FA8, #301E4C)`,light:`#4C2E96 / #7A57E0 / #6A4BD0 / #5A3FA3 / #2A1A5E`,lightSwatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #4C2E96, #7A57E0, #6A4BD0, #5A3FA3, #2A1A5E)`,note:`The five gradient stops, brightest to deepest on dark. --brand-shade is a colour stop; --wash (Surfaces) is a tint — do not swap them. Utilities: text-brand, bg-brand/40, border-brand-hi…`,agent:`Gradient and glow stops are the --brand-hi, --brand, --brand-mid, --brand-deep and --brand-shade tokens (utilities text-brand, bg-brand, border-brand-hi and so on). Use them in a named ns-* block in src/styles.css, never a hex at a call site. For an alpha, write color-mix(in oklab, var(--brand) 40%, transparent), never rgba().`},{name:`--trace-head / --on-brand / --ink-shadow`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`#FFFFFF / #FFFFFF / #000000`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #FFFFFF 0 50%, #000000 50%)`,light:`#3B2280 / #FFFFFF / #301E4C`,lightSwatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #3B2280 0 50%, #301E4C 50%)`,note:`--trace-head is the bright head of a border trace or lit rim (white on dark, deep violet on light). --on-brand is text over a brand-filled surface (always white). --ink-shadow is the colour every drop shadow is mixed from.`,agent:`A hover trace, lit rim or highlight head uses var(--trace-head). Text on a --brand fill uses text-on-brand. Drop shadows are the named shadow-lift, shadow-wash, shadow-capture, shadow-brand, shadow-brand-lift and shadow-brand-far utilities (@theme in src/styles.css), each mixed from --ink-shadow or --brand — never shadow-[…rgba(…)] at a call site.`},{name:`--cat-pink … --cat-green`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/sections/AgentCatalogue.tsx`,value:`#F472B6 / #FB923C / #60A5FA / #2DD4BF / #FACC15 / #C084FC / #818CF8 / #4ADE80`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #F472B6 0.0% 12.5%, #FB923C 12.5% 25.0%, #60A5FA 25.0% 37.5%, #2DD4BF 37.5% 50.0%, #FACC15 50.0% 62.5%, #C084FC 62.5% 75.0%, #818CF8 75.0% 87.5%, #4ADE80 87.5% 100.0%)`,light:`#DB2777 / #EA580C / #2563EB / #0F766E / #A16207 / #9333EA / #4F46E5 / #15803D`,lightSwatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #DB2777 0.0% 12.5%, #EA580C 12.5% 25.0%, #2563EB 25.0% 37.5%, #0F766E 37.5% 50.0%, #A16207 50.0% 62.5%, #9333EA 62.5% 75.0%, #4F46E5 75.0% 87.5%, #15803D 87.5% 100.0%)`,note:`One hue per agent category on the /marketplace/ cards, matching the product's Marketplace tab (pink, orange, blue, teal, yellow, purple, indigo); --cat-green is the "Ready to use" pill. The card's top strip, wash, icon tile and category label all mix from the one hue.`,agent:`Agent cards take their colour from the category → --cat-* map in AgentCatalogue.tsx (CATEGORY_STYLE). Never use these hues for anything but a catalogue category or the ready pill.`},{name:`--land-glow / --land-halo`,path:`src/styles.css (ns-intro-land)`,value:`#C9B8F0 / #A78BFA`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #C9B8F0 0 50%, #A78BFA 50%)`,light:`#8B6CF0 / #B9A4F7`,lightSwatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #8B6CF0 0 50%, #B9A4F7 50%)`,note:`The glow on the navbar logo as the intro lands on it: a tight core and a wide halo. A smooth purple on light, where the inverted --brand-hi read as a dark smudge.`,agent:`The intro hand-over pulse (@keyframes ns-intro-land) glows with var(--land-glow) (core) and var(--land-halo) (halo). Change the landing colour here, not in the keyframes.`},{name:`--face-shade / --face-lift`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/primitives/DemoButton.tsx`,value:`#301E4C / #6B4FA8`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #301E4C 0 50%, #6B4FA8 50%)`,light:`#C9B8F0 / #EDE7FA`,lightSwatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #C9B8F0 0 50%, #EDE7FA 50%)`,note:`The DemoButton face at rest: two inset bevel tints on a nav-bg pill, label in --foreground. On hover the brand flood is painted over the whole border box so no second ring shows.`,agent:`The primary CTA face is DemoButton; its bevel is the --face-shade / --face-lift insets. Do not restyle it at a call site — a second primary look is a new primitive next to it, with its own tokens in both theme blocks.`},{name:`--logo-ink`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/sections/PartnerMarquee.tsx`,value:`1 (white silhouettes)`,light:`0 (black silhouettes)`,note:`A number, not a colour: .ns-logo is filter: brightness(0) invert(var(--logo-ink)). The 28 pre-whitened partner WebPs need no light-mode asset.`,agent:`Partner wordmarks are the .ns-logo class, which flattens any logo to the page's ink via --logo-ink; do not ship a second set of logo files for light mode.`},{name:`--aurora-* / --particle* / --fracture-*`,path:`src/styles.css · src/lib/theme.ts`,value:`base #000000 · wash #301E4C · lift #3D2A5E · violet #A78BFA · irid #332654→#8B6ED9 · particle #6B52B3/#C7B8F5 · fracture #8B5CF6 on #000000`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #000000, #301E4C, #A78BFA, #8B6ED9)`,light:`base #FAF9FC · wash #6B4FA8 · lift #CBBDF0 · violet #7A57E0 · irid #4C2E96→#9B7FF0 · particle #7A57E0/#4C2E96 · fracture #8B5CF6 on #000000 (unchanged)`,lightSwatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, #FAF9FC, #CBBDF0, #9B7FF0, #7A57E0)`,note:`The GL palettes. Registered with @property as <color> so a light-dark() value computes to a colour JS can read; hex on purpose. Each canvas re-reads and re-uploads on a theme flip, nothing remounts.`,agent:`A WebGL layer never writes a colour in GLSL or JS: declare uniform vec3s, read the --aurora-* / --particle* / --fracture-* tokens (or add new ones under the Theme heading, registered with @property syntax "<color>") with readThemeTokens() + hexToRgb() from src/lib/theme.ts, upload at mount, and re-upload from a useEffect keyed on useTheme().resolved. See SiteBackground.tsx for the pattern.`}]},{title:`Gradients and glow utilities`,description:`Every stop comes from the wash purple family. Each is a named utility in styles.css — never re-declared at a call site.`,entries:[{name:`ns-accent-text (hero headline fill)`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`ns-accent-text`,swatch:`radial-gradient(ellipse at 20% 20%, var(--brand-hi) 0%, transparent 55%), radial-gradient(ellipse at 80% 30%, var(--brand) 0%, transparent 60%), radial-gradient(ellipse at 50% 90%, var(--brand-shade) 0%, transparent 65%), linear-gradient(90deg, var(--brand-mid) 0%, var(--brand-deep) 100%)`,note:`The purple fill on the accent line of every hero headline — homepage <Hero> and every <PageHero>. One definition recolours every hero.`,agent:`The hero headline accent is the ns-accent-text utility in src/styles.css (three ellipses in --brand-hi / --brand / --brand-shade over linear-gradient(90deg, var(--brand-mid), var(--brand-deep)), clipped to text; the tokens carry the light values). Apply the class; never re-declare the gradient at a call site. Keep pb-[0.14em] on the element so descenders are not clipped.`},{name:`ns-ink-accent-text (ink → brand text fill)`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`ns-ink-accent-text`,swatch:`linear-gradient(90deg, var(--foreground) 0%, var(--foreground) 30%, var(--brand) 75%, var(--brand-hi) 100%)`,note:`The quieter sibling of ns-accent-text: page ink running into brand purple, for a short line that reads as copy first — the <WordFlip> line on /platform/seek. Follows the theme through --foreground.`,agent:`For a text fill that starts in the page ink and ends in brand, use the ns-ink-accent-text utility in src/styles.css (linear-gradient(90deg, var(--foreground) 0–30%, var(--brand) 75%, var(--brand-hi) 100%), clipped to text). Apply the class with pb-[0.14em]; never re-declare it at a call site.`},{name:`site-gradient-bg`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`site-gradient-bg`,swatch:`var(--surface) radial-gradient(800px 600px at 50% 50%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--wash) 18%, transparent) 0%, transparent 70%)`,note:`Surface base with one soft central wash. Scroll-attached, never fixed.`,agent:`Use the site-gradient-bg utility for washed section backgrounds: #2A2638 base with a single soft radial wash. Keep background-attachment: scroll — fixed reintroduces scroll jank.`},{name:`site-gradient-mesh`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`site-gradient-mesh`,swatch:`var(--surface) radial-gradient(1100px 900px at 18% 8%, var(--wash) 0%, transparent 55%), radial-gradient(900px 750px at 82% 26%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--wash) 72%, transparent) 0%, transparent 55%), radial-gradient(1300px 850px at 8% 72%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--wash) 55%, transparent) 0%, transparent 60%), radial-gradient(1050px 900px at 92% 96%, var(--wash) 0%, transparent 55%)`,note:`Five-point wash for richer full-bleed sections.`,agent:`Use the site-gradient-mesh utility for a richer five-point washed background. Every stop is --wash at some color-mix alpha; keep background-attachment: scroll.`},{name:`ns-glow-edge / ns-glow-edge-soft`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`ns-glow-edge`,swatch:`linear-gradient(135deg, var(--brand) 0%, var(--brand-shade) 60%, var(--surface) 100%)`,note:`A gradient ring masked to the border of a card, at inset -1px so it sits ON the card's border-hairline rather than one pixel inside it (RULES.md C15). Used on the Platform carousel cards and <GlassCard halo>.`,agent:`For a glowing card edge use the ns-glow-edge or ns-glow-edge-soft utility from src/styles.css (a masked gradient ring on ::after). Do not combine either with ns-border-trace — both claim the pseudo-elements. Any new masked ring is placed at inset: -1px, never 0, or it doubles the card's hairline border (RULES.md C15).`},{name:`ns-glow-edge-track`,path:`src/styles.css · src/hooks/use-pointer-glow.ts`,value:`ns-glow-edge-track`,swatch:`radial-gradient(circle at 30% 30%, var(--trace-head) 0%, var(--surface) 60%)`,note:`The same ring, lit only where the pointer is — aimed through --glow-x / --glow-y by usePointerGlow. Sovereignty card.`,agent:`For a pointer-tracked glowing edge pair the ns-glow-edge-track utility with the usePointerGlow hook (src/hooks/use-pointer-glow.ts), which springs --glow-x and --glow-y toward the cursor. Defaults rest the light at centre so prerendered HTML and reduced motion get a finished card.`},{name:`ns-glow-bleed`,path:`src/styles.css · src/hooks/use-pointer-glow.ts`,value:`ns-glow-bleed`,swatch:`radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, var(--brand-hi) 0%, var(--canvas) 70%)`,note:`A blurred gradient behind an element that bleeds past its edges. Customer-story film tile. The element must not clip overflow.`,agent:`For light bleeding out past an element's edges use the ns-glow-bleed utility with usePointerGlow. The element must not set overflow-hidden, or there is nothing to see.`},{name:`ns-marquee-mask`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`ns-marquee-mask`,swatch:`linear-gradient(to right, var(--canvas) 0%, var(--brand-hi) 12%, var(--brand-hi) 88%, var(--canvas) 100%)`,note:`Edge fade for horizontally scrolling strips. Uses overflow-x: clip so the overhang cannot widen the page.`,agent:`Fade the edges of a horizontally scrolling strip with the ns-marquee-mask utility. Never fade edges with a stacked opaque overlay div, and never change its overflow-x: clip to hidden.`},{name:`DemoButton hover flood`,path:`src/components/primitives/DemoButton.tsx`,value:`linear-gradient(180deg, var(--brand) 0%, var(--brand-deep) 45%, var(--brand-shade) 72%, var(--brand-hi) 100%)`,swatch:`linear-gradient(180deg, var(--brand) 0%, var(--brand-deep) 45%, var(--brand-shade) 72%, var(--brand-hi) 100%)`,note:`Fades in over the nav-bg face on hover, text going to --on-brand, plus a bloom shadow in --brand. Lives inside the primitive only.`,agent:`The primary CTA hover flood belongs to DemoButton only. Do not clone its gradient or box-shadow into another component — use the primitive.`}]}],r=[{title:`Families`,description:`Two families, both self-hosted from @fontsource-variable with the latin subset only (RULES.md A1). Never a third, never a Google Fonts link.`,entries:[{name:`font-sans — Open Sans Variable`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`font-sans`,note:`All UI and prose. Weights 300–800. Preloaded in __root.tsx; a metric-matched Arial fallback ("Open Sans Fallback") keeps the swap from moving text.`,previewClassName:`font-sans text-h2`,agent:`Use font-sans (Open Sans Variable, self-hosted) for all UI and prose. Never introduce another sans family, and never load a font from fonts.googleapis.com. Keep the "Open Sans Fallback" @font-face in src/styles.css in the stack right after Open Sans, and recompute its size-adjust and overrides if the font file changes.`},{name:`font-mono — JetBrains Mono Variable`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`font-mono`,note:`Eyebrows, small labels, code and token values. The only place uppercase is allowed.`,previewClassName:`font-mono text-body`,agent:`Use font-mono (JetBrains Mono Variable, self-hosted) for eyebrows, small labels, code and token values. Eyebrows are the only place the uppercase utility is allowed.`}]},{title:`Scale`,description:`Sizes are tokens (--text-* in @theme). No arbitrary text-[…] values (RULES.md C3).`,entries:[{name:`text-display`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`text-display`,note:`3.25rem / 1.08 — section display headlines.`,previewClassName:`text-display font-semibold`,agent:`Use text-display (3.25rem, line-height 1.08) for section display headlines.`},{name:`text-h1`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`text-h1`,note:`2.5rem / 1.1 — page titles.`,previewClassName:`text-h1 font-semibold`,agent:`Use text-h1 (2.5rem, line-height 1.1) for page titles.`},{name:`text-h2`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`text-h2`,note:`1.75rem / 1.2 — sub-headings and card titles.`,previewClassName:`text-h2 font-semibold`,agent:`Use text-h2 (1.75rem, line-height 1.2) for sub-headings and card titles.`},{name:`text-lede`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`text-lede`,note:`1.3125rem / 1.5 — the intro paragraph under a headline.`,previewClassName:`text-lede`,agent:`Use text-lede (1.3125rem, line-height 1.5) for the intro paragraph under a headline.`},{name:`text-body`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`text-body`,note:`1rem / 1.6 — default paragraph.`,previewClassName:`text-body`,agent:`Use text-body (1rem, line-height 1.6) for default paragraphs.`},{name:`text-small`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`text-small`,note:`0.8125rem / 1.5 — nav links, captions, button labels.`,previewClassName:`text-small`,agent:`Use text-small (0.8125rem, line-height 1.5) for nav links, captions and button labels.`}]},{title:`Conventions`,entries:[{name:`Sentence case headings`,value:`Pricing for cloud and on-prem.`,note:`No uppercase on h1/h2. Uppercase only on mono eyebrows.`,agent:`Write every h1 and h2 in sentence case. Do not apply the uppercase utility to headings — uppercase is reserved for small font-mono eyebrows.`},{name:`Copy lives in content/`,path:`src/content/`,value:`import { sovereigntyPage as s } from "@/content/sovereignty";`,note:`Strings arrive in a route from a content module and reach sections as props. Sections own no copy (RULES.md C4).`,agent:`Put every user-facing string in a module under src/content/ and pass it into sections as props. A section never contains its own copy, and content/ imports nothing (RULES.md C2, C4).`}]}],i=[{title:`WebGL figures`,description:`All built in ogl (bundled, self-hosted); no third-party WebGL runtime, ever (RULES.md G2) — nothing here fetches from another origin. One GL context runs for the page; a figure adds a second only while its section is on screen (C8). Every canvas goes through lib/gl-quality.ts for its size and its right to exist.`,entries:[{name:`gl-quality — budget, watchdog, renderer gate`,path:`src/lib/gl-quality.ts`,value:`pickDpr(w, h, BUDGET.x) · classifyRenderer(gl) · createFrameWatchdog({ … })`,note:`Drawing buffers are sized to a pixel budget, not a DPR cap. A rAF-delta watchdog drops quality instantly and recovers slowly. A software renderer (SwiftShader, llvmpipe) turns every canvas off for the session via useMotionAllowed.`,agent:`Any new WebGL canvas must: size its renderer with pickDpr(w, h, BUDGET.<name>) from src/lib/gl-quality.ts (add a budget there), check renderer.gl and classifyRenderer(gl) === 'software' right after creating the context and bail to the still if so, and — if it is fill-bound — feed createFrameWatchdog a sample() per rendered frame with a ladder that ends in a complete picture. See SiteBackground.tsx for the full pattern and FractureCore.tsx for the buffer-scale-only one.`},{name:`SiteBackground — the aurora`,path:`src/components/media/SiteBackground.tsx · src/components/layout/AmbientBackground.tsx`,value:`<AmbientBackground />`,note:`Fixed behind every section, mounted once from __root. Lazy-loaded only once useMotionAllowed says yes. 1.4 M-pixel budget; ladder 1 → 0.75 → 0.5 buffer scale → 24 steps → stop (CSS wash stays). Stops drawing (context kept) on a hidden tab and while a DarkBand veil is opaque (lib/ambient.ts); not fetched at all while a visitor who landed mid-band is under it.`,agent:`The ambient aurora is src/components/media/SiteBackground.tsx, mounted once by AmbientBackground in __root.tsx behind every page. Never mount a second one, and never scope it to a hero — the page goes flat below the first viewport. Its rAF loop runs only when !document.hidden && !ambientPaused() (src/lib/ambient.ts, set by DarkBand); pause by cancelling the loop, never by unmounting.`},{name:`SovereigntyFigure — fracture core`,path:`src/components/media/SovereigntyFigure.tsx · src/components/media/FractureCore.tsx`,value:`<SovereigntyFigure className="aspect-square w-full max-w-[640px]" />`,note:`Eight cubes closing into one around a violet core. A still (public/img/sovereignty-core.*) is always painted first; the canvas mounts only near the viewport.`,agent:`The sovereignty figure is SovereigntyFigure, which paints a still <img> first and lazy-mounts FractureCore (ogl) only while the section is near the viewport. Keep the still — it is the reduced-motion, save-data and no-WebGL path, and the LCP-eligible element (RULES.md B7, C9).`},{name:`TrustFigure / TrustHeroFigure — point cloud`,path:`src/components/media/TrustFigure.tsx · TrustHeroFigure.tsx · TrustParticles.tsx`,value:`<TrustFigure … /> · <TrustHeroFigure … />`,note:"~7,000-point cloud morphing between forms (media/form-registry.ts: trust-forms.ts + page-forms.ts). TrustFigure is scrubbed by scroll, over one still per chapter (trust-container / -layers / -fanout, each in -light and -dark, the nearest one showing until the canvas paints); TrustHeroFigure resolves and holds as a page opener — /trust/*, and since 2026-09-23 the eight Resources pages, the four /solutions/ pages, /partners/, /pricing/, /contact/ and /compare/, plus the `icon` form (the NeuralSeek mark) as the ContainerHub core on /aws/, /azure/, /ibm-cloud/ — the one form allowed on more than one page (one shape each, stills at public/img/point-<form>-{light,dark}, swapped by ThemedStill). Map: docs/POINT-FIGURES.md; every figure with its fallback: content/figures.ts, rendered above.",agent:"The trust point cloud is TrustParticles (ogl), loaded only through TrustFigure (scroll-scrubbed) or TrustHeroFigure (page opener). Forms live in trust-forms.ts (homepage, /trust/*) and page-forms.ts (Resources pages), merged in form-registry.ts. One figure, one meaning: never reuse a form on a second page — add one (docs/POINT-FIGURES.md says how, including the y-down gotcha and the still shoot). Never import TrustParticles directly — it must stay out of the initial chunk. TrustFigure takes `stills` (one per chapter form, public/img/trust-<form>); every new figure gets a row in src/content/figures.ts with its fallback."},{name:`CapabilityGlyph`,path:`src/components/media/CapabilityGlyph.tsx`,value:`<CapabilityGlyph variant="…" />`,note:`SVG node/line illustrations for the capability cards, animated by the glyph-* keyframes.`,agent:`Capability card art is CapabilityGlyph — an inline SVG per variant, animated by the glyph-drift / glyph-pulse / glyph-flow / glyph-breathe classes in src/styles.css. Add a variant there rather than a new illustration component.`}]},{title:`Media`,entries:[{name:`Picture`,path:`src/components/media/Picture.tsx`,value:`<Picture name="…" alt="…" width={…} height={…} priority />`,note:`AVIF with a WebP fallback, explicit width/height always. Derivatives are built by bun run media from gitignored _incoming-assets/.`,agent:`Render images with <Picture name alt width height /> — AVIF plus WebP, explicit dimensions to hold CLS. Commit only the optimised derivatives; originals stay in the gitignored _incoming-assets/ (RULES.md A12).`},{name:`ThemedStill`,path:`src/components/media/ThemedStill.tsx`,value:`<ThemedStill name="point-skyline" className="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full" />`,note:`A point-cloud figure's fallback in both themes: <name>-light and <name>-dark, swapped by .ns-only-light / .ns-only-dark (OS or the per-tab toggle). Both lazy, so the hidden one is never fetched. Used by TrustHeroFigure and TrustFigure.`,agent:`A figure's fallback still is decorative and themed: render it with <ThemedStill name="<stem>" /> (src/components/media/ThemedStill.tsx), which needs public/img/<stem>-light and <stem>-dark (.avif + .webp). Shoot both with _private/stills/shoot-themes.mjs; scripts/qa/stills.mjs fails the build if either is missing. Never give it priority — an eager hidden image is still fetched.`},{name:`TiltStage`,path:`src/components/media/TiltStage.tsx`,value:`<TiltStage image="…" alt="…" width={…} height={…} priority className="min-h-[420px]" />`,note:`A product capture standing in 3D: tilted in perspective, leaning toward the cursor, over a blurred blob and a glass back panel, inside the pointer-tracked glow rim. The Platform carousel's stage and the /platform/ hero figure. Optional chip; zoom={false} is the flat rail variant; frame={false} drops the box and rim so the capture fills the stage (the /platform/ hero).`,agent:`Show a product capture in 3D with <TiltStage image alt width height /> (src/components/media/TiltStage.tsx) — it is the stage inside the Platform carousel cards and the /platform/ hero. Pass priority when it is the hero figure, chip={{ tag, label, icon }} for the pinned label, zoom={false} for a flat rail, frame={false} for the boxless hero variant. Do not rebuild the perspective/tilt inline.`},{name:`PartnerLogo`,path:`src/components/media/PartnerLogo.tsx`,value:`<PartnerLogo src="/img/marks/aws.svg" name="Amazon Web Services" width={280} height={280} priority />`,note:`A partner page's opener figure: the NeuralSeek + partner lockup (NeuralSeek on top, a short rule, the partner's mark below at the same ink area), still, in the flat .ns-logo ink (white on dark, black on light, 78%) — the homepage logo strip's treatment. /aws/, /azure/, /ibm-cloud/ and /edelta/ (/ibm-fusion-partnership/ shows the appliance instead — PartnerAppliance). Width per logo, for equal optical weight; the box keeps TiltStage's height.`,agent:`For the hero figure of a partner page use <PartnerLogo {...p.hero.logo} priority /> with a mark from public/img/marks/ and a per-logo width/height in the content module (PartnerHeroLogo in content/partners/cloud-page.ts). Never recolour a vendor logo; .ns-logo flattens it.`},{name:`Video`,path:`src/components/media/Video.tsx`,value:`<Video id="…" … />`,note:`Silent looping capture with a visible pause control (data-video-toggle). Poster first, video preload=none, mounts in view only.`,agent:`Autoplaying captures use <Video>, which renders the required pause control (data-video-toggle) — check-output.mjs fails a page with a <video> and no control (WCAG 2.2.2, RULES.md B6). Never write a bare <video autoplay>.`},{name:`FilmLightbox`,path:`src/components/media/FilmLightbox.tsx`,value:`<FilmLightbox … />`,note:`The customer-story film in a dialog with native controls. Mounts nothing while closed.`,agent:`Long-form film with sound opens in FilmLightbox — a dialog with native controls that mounts nothing until opened. Do not autoplay a film with speech inline.`}]},{title:`Motion inventory`,description:`Every effect is a reusable pair: one component or hook plus one named ns-* block in styles.css (RULES.md C12). Timing lives in the CSS block. Every block has its own prefers-reduced-motion override. Reuse before adding.`,entries:[{name:`<Reveal>`,path:`src/components/layout/Reveal.tsx · .ns-reveal`,value:`<Reveal>…</Reveal>`,note:`A block easing in the first time it scrolls into view. Hidden state applied from JS after mount — prerendered HTML never ships invisible content.`,agent:`For a section that eases in on first scroll into view, wrap it in <Reveal> (src/components/layout/Reveal.tsx). Do not write a transition or animate-* class at the call site (RULES.md C12).`},{name:`Page transition`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`@view-transition { navigation: auto; }`,note:`Every internal link is a plain <a> (RULES.md C14), so a navigation is a real document load. Without this, Chrome's paint-holding keeps the OLD page on screen until the new one paints — the homepage briefly flashing before a deep page loads is that hold, not a redirect. This crossfades root instead. Reduced motion turns navigation off (instant swap).`,agent:`Do not add a per-page fade-in for navigation — the crossfade lives once in the root Page transition block in styles.css (@view-transition, ::view-transition-old(root) / ::view-transition-new(root)). It already composes with the router's defaultViewTransition for same-document navigations.`},{name:`<HotspotFigure> / <CardExplorer> / <CardRail>`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/ · .ns-hotspot*, .ns-explore*, .ns-rail*`,value:`data-active + --ns-hs-cx/cy/rx/ry · aria-pressed · scroll-snap`,note:`The platform pages' interactive sections (2026-09-17). Hotspot: registered custom properties glide the spotlight between regions. Explore: the active card lifts, the panel body remounts and fades. Rail: smooth scroll-behavior, auto under reduced motion. All three ship a finished still state.`,agent:`The three interactive platform sections keep every visible part of their motion in their ns-* blocks; a page wanting a variant asks for one there, never a transition at the call site (RULES.md C12).`},{name:`useScrollActive`,path:`src/hooks/use-scroll-active.ts`,value:`const ref = useScrollActive(".ns-hotspot-item", (i) => setActive(items[i].id))`,note:`Which row is the reader on: reports the last row whose top crossed 45% of the viewport, one rAF per scroll. HotspotFigure's driver — there is no hover on that section, scroll and focus only.`,agent:`For a list whose companion figure should follow the reader's scroll, use useScrollActive(selector, onChange) — never a second scroll listener per row, and do not add hover on top of it.`},{name:`useInView`,path:`src/hooks/use-in-view.ts`,value:`const { ref, inView } = useInView(undefined, { once: true })`,note:`IntersectionObserver hook. once:true fires and disconnects; once:false tracks both edges.`,agent:`For any "do X when this is on screen" trigger use the useInView hook (src/hooks/use-in-view.ts): useInView(rootMargin?, { once, enabled, threshold }) — once: true for a single fire, once: false to track leave as well. Do not create another IntersectionObserver.`},{name:`useMotionAllowed`,path:`src/hooks/use-motion-allowed.ts`,value:`const allowed = useMotionAllowed()`,note:`May I mount a canvas or heavy animation for this visitor? Reduced motion, save-data, low-end and no-WebGL all say no; the caller has already painted a complete fallback.`,agent:`Gate every canvas or heavy animation on useMotionAllowed(), and paint the complete still or CSS fallback first — every "no" is a finished state, not a degraded one (RULES.md B7).`},{name:`<DarkBand>`,path:`src/components/layout/DarkBand.tsx · .ns-band*`,value:`<DarkBand>…</DarkBand>`,note:`The scroll-linked light → dark → light veil over the middle of a page: opens right after the hero (fully dark as it leaves), closes right before the CTA. The fall is --ns-band-fall (60vh) on every device. Opaque at the peak, where the aurora stops drawing. Its veil is position: fixed.`,agent:`Lower the lights over the middle of a page with <DarkBand>, opening it directly after the PageHero/Hero and closing it directly before the <Cta /> or form — any section left between hero and band stays light. A band much under two viewports tall cannot reach full dark. Never wrap DarkBand in anything that sets opacity or transform (including <Reveal>) — its veil is position: fixed and would lose the viewport.`},{name:`.ns-marquee*`,path:`src/styles.css · PartnerMarquee.tsx · WorkflowCarousel.tsx`,value:`ns-marquee ns-marquee-track ns-marquee-mask`,note:`An infinite horizontal strip, paused on hover, static under reduced motion.`,agent:`For an infinite horizontal strip reuse the ns-marquee / ns-marquee-track / ns-marquee-mask classes as PartnerMarquee does. Do not write a new @keyframes for scrolling content.`},{name:`.ns-border-trace`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/primitives/GhostButton.tsx`,value:`ns-border-trace`,note:`A lit segment walking the border on hover, with a bright head. Registered --ns-trace-angle so the sweep interpolates.`,agent:`The hover treatment for a secondary control is the ns-border-trace utility, applied inside GhostButton. Do not combine it with ns-glow-edge, and keep the element's own background transparent — the halo sits at z-index -1.`},{name:`ns-brand-fill`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`<p className="ns-brand-fill text-on-brand …">`,note:`A surface in the brand gradient (--brand-deep → --brand-shade, a --brand glow in one corner) that white text clears AA on in both themes. The chat's bubbles, avatar and send control; the chat header is the same fill plus a faint masked line grid (.ns-chat-head). ns-on-brand-glass is a control sitting on it.`,agent:`For a brand-coloured surface carrying text use className="ns-brand-fill text-on-brand"; never a hand-written gradient or a pale brand stop under white text.`},{name:`chat-mark`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/chat/ParticleMark.tsx · SiteChat.tsx`,value:`<ParticleMark busy={pending} className="size-9" />`,note:"The chat icon: the real mark in grey at rest; on launcher hover/focus it dissolves into coloured particles (one SVG dot per filled cell of a 30×30 grid, no WebGL, RULES C8) that fly out, spiral back into the mark and fade as they land, handing back to the mark in the brand colour — never both at once; `busy` makes the mark breathe while an answer streams. The block also owns the launcher's show/hide and the panel's open/close (grows from the launcher's corner with a blur clearing; exits the same way, quicker — ChatPanel sets data-closing and closes on animationend) and its backdrop. Reduced motion: all static.",agent:`To show the NeuralSeek mark as particles without a canvas use <ParticleMark /> (src/components/chat/ParticleMark.tsx). Its timing is the chat-mark block in src/styles.css — tune it there, never at a call site (RULES.md C12).`},{name:`ns-tilt-stage`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/media/TiltStage.tsx`,value:`ns-tilt-stage · ns-tilt-stage-blob`,note:`The timing of the 3D stage: a slow ease back to rest, a tighter follow while the pointer is over it (data-tracking), and the blob's fade. Reduced motion holds the resting tilt with no transition.`,agent:`The 3D capture stage's timing is the ns-tilt-stage block in src/styles.css — TiltStage only sets the transform and toggles data-tracking. Tune the curves there, never in the component or at a call site (RULES.md C12).`},{name:`.ns-steps`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/sections/StepList.tsx`,value:`ns-steps · ns-steps-in · ns-step-rail`,note:`The rail under each step badge scaling in from the top once the list is in view. Armed from JS after mount; at rest with no transition under reduced motion.`,agent:`The step rail's timing is the Steps block in src/styles.css; StepList only toggles ns-steps / ns-steps-in. Tune the curve there, never at a call site (RULES.md C12).`},{name:`usePointerGlow`,path:`src/hooks/use-pointer-glow.ts`,value:`const ref = usePointerGlow<HTMLDivElement>(active)`,note:`Springs --glow-x / --glow-y toward the cursor. Pairs with ns-glow-edge-track and ns-glow-bleed.`,agent:`Aim a gradient at the cursor with the usePointerGlow hook, which springs --glow-x and --glow-y on the element; pair it with ns-glow-edge-track or ns-glow-bleed. Do not track the pointer with React state.`},{name:`Reduced motion`,path:`src/styles.css`,value:`@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`,note:`Every ns-* block carries its own override under the same heading, at rest with no transition.`,agent:`Every new motion block in src/styles.css gets its own prefers-reduced-motion: reduce override under the same /* ---- name ---- */ heading, rendering at rest with no transition. Then add the effect to the inventory table in CLAUDE.md and the Figures & motion page of this guide.`}]},{title:`Showcase blocks`,description:`Added 2026-09-14 with the Cards, Text motion and Buttons pages of this guide. Each is one component or hook plus one named block in styles.css, with its own reduced-motion override (RULES.md C12). The live demos are those three pages.`,entries:[{name:`ns-motion-pause`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/layout/MotionToggle.tsx`,value:`[data-motion="paused"]`,note:`The WCAG 2.2.2 pause control (RULES.md B6). <MotionToggle> sets data-motion="paused" on <html>; every CSS animation freezes in place.`,agent:`Any page that loops motion for more than five seconds mounts <MotionToggle /> (src/components/layout/MotionToggle.tsx). It pauses every animation via [data-motion="paused"] in styles.css; do not build a second pause mechanism.`},{name:`ThemeToggle`,path:`src/components/layout/ThemeToggle.tsx · src/components/primitives/ToggleButton.tsx`,value:`<ThemeToggle />`,note:`The light/dark switch: a ToggleButton with an icon face (sun while dark, moon while light; aria-pressed = dark on). Mounted in the navbar, the mobile sheet and this sidebar. Session only, nothing stored.`,agent:`The theme switch is <ThemeToggle /> from src/components/layout/ThemeToggle.tsx; it already sits in SiteHeader. Do not build another toggle, and do not persist its state (RULES.md B1). For an icon-only two-state control elsewhere, use ToggleButton with its icon prop.`},{name:`ns-rim`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/cards/GlassCard.tsx`,value:`ns-rim`,note:`One-pixel light along a glass panel's top edge, brightening on hover or focus-within. A child element, not a pseudo, so it composes with ns-glow-edge-soft.`,agent:`For the top rim light on a glass panel use <GlassCard> or its ns-rim child element from src/styles.css; never redeclare the gradient at a call site.`},{name:`ns-tilt`,path:`src/styles.css · src/hooks/use-tilt.ts · src/components/cards/TiltCard.tsx`,value:`ns-tilt · ns-tilt-layer`,note:`Generic content tilt: useTilt writes --ns-tilt-x/-y, the block applies perspective and shares TiltStage's two curves. Fine-pointer only; touch gets a flat card.`,agent:`To tilt a card toward the pointer use <TiltCard> or pair useTilt (src/hooks/use-tilt.ts) with the ns-tilt utility; lift a child with ns-tilt-layer. Timing stays in the styles.css block.`},{name:`ns-spotlight`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/cards/SpotlightCard.tsx`,value:`ns-spotlight · ns-spotlight-wash`,note:`An inner radial pool of light aimed through --glow-x / --glow-y, on top of the existing ns-glow-edge-track rim. Every <PointGrid> item is one (as="li" size="lg") since 2026-09-15. Since Oct 6 2026 the rim, like the wash, shows only on the hovered or focused card with a mouse (in a grid all of them used to lean at once); touch keeps the static centred rim.`,agent:`For a cursor spotlight inside a card use <SpotlightCard as="article|li" size="md|lg">, which composes usePointerGlow, ns-glow-edge-track and the ns-spotlight wash. PointGrid already renders its items through it. Do not write a new radial-gradient at the call site.`},{name:`ns-conic-ring`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/cards/ConicRingCard.tsx`,value:`ns-conic-ring · --ns-ring-speed`,note:`Always-on rotating conic border with a blurred halo (--ns-ring-angle is registered). Loops, so the page needs <MotionToggle>. Reduced motion: static three-stop ring.`,agent:`For an always-on glowing border use <ConicRingCard> or the ns-conic-ring utility; tune speed with --ns-ring-speed. It loops, so mount <MotionToggle> on that page (RULES.md B6).`},{name:`ns-lift · ns-sheen`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/cards/LiftCard.tsx`,value:`ns-lift · ns-lift-rule · ns-sheen`,note:`Hover lift (4px), glow border and shadow, an eyebrow rule drawn with scaleX, and a diagonal light sweep. All on :focus-within too.`,agent:`For a hover-lift card with a light sweep use <LiftCard> or the ns-lift / ns-sheen utilities from src/styles.css; never a hover:-translate-y or transition-* class at the call site.`},{name:`ns-volume`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/cards/VolumeCard.tsx`,value:`ns-volume · ns-volume-blob-{a,b,c} · ns-volume-grid`,note:`Three blurred radial blobs drifting on 11/13/15s with a faint dot grid — the volumetric gradient panel. Loops, so it needs <MotionToggle>. Reduced motion: still blobs.`,agent:`For a volumetric gradient background use <VolumeCard> or the ns-volume utilities; tune dots with --ns-volume-dot / --ns-volume-pitch. It loops, so mount <MotionToggle> on that page.`},{name:`ns-split`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/layout/SplitReveal.tsx`,value:`ns-split · ns-split-in · ns-split-chars · ns-split-lines`,note:`Staggered per-unit reveal; each unit's delay is --i × --ns-split-stagger (40ms words, 18ms chars). Armed after mount like <Reveal>; prerendered HTML is fully visible.`,agent:`For a staggered text entrance use <SplitReveal by="word|char|line" as="p|h2|h3|span">. Stagger and duration live in the ns-split block in styles.css, not in props. entrance="load" is the hero variant (ns-split-load): a stylesheet animation from first paint that never hides the text, so it may sit on the LCP; PageHero already uses it — do not add a second kinetic heading on the same page. label="parent" drops SplitReveal's own accessible copy (aria-label / sr-only span) when the caller names the enclosing element itself — PageHero puts aria-label on its h1 — so text extractors that ignore aria-hidden read each line once.`},{name:`ns-shimmer`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/layout/TextShimmer.tsx`,value:`ns-shimmer · ns-shimmer-once · --ns-shimmer-speed · --ns-shimmer-once-speed · --ns-shimmer-once-delay`,note:`A bright band sweeping across ns-accent-text. ns-shimmer loops (needs <MotionToggle>); ns-shimmer-once sweeps a single time after paint and rests — every <PageHero> accent line uses it. Reduced motion falls back to the static accent fill in both.`,agent:"For an animated headline gradient use <TextShimmer>, which layers ns-shimmer on ns-accent-text; pass `once` for a single sweep after mount (what <PageHero> does — no pause control needed). Never redeclare the accent gradient; the looping form needs <MotionToggle> on that page."},{name:`ns-blur-in`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/layout/BlurIn.tsx`,value:`ns-blur-in · ns-blur-in-show`,note:`Block reveal from blur(12px) and 8px below, 900ms on the house curve. Same arming pattern as <Reveal>.`,agent:`For a blur-to-sharp entrance wrap the block in <BlurIn>. Use <Reveal> for a plain slide-up; both arm after mount so prerendered HTML stays visible.`},{name:`ns-count`,path:`src/styles.css · src/hooks/use-count-up.ts · src/components/layout/CountUp.tsx`,value:`ns-count · --ns-count-ch`,note:`A statistic counting up on in-view (rAF, ease-out-quint, 1400ms). Width reserved in ch so neighbours never move; SSR and reduced motion show the final value.`,agent:`For an animated statistic use <CountUp value decimals prefix suffix />. Duration is a hook default in use-count-up.ts, not a prop.`},{name:`ns-line-draw`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/layout/LineDraw.tsx`,value:`ns-line-draw · ns-line-draw-hover · ns-line-draw-in`,note:`A rule drawn left to right with scaleX, on hover / focus-visible / .group hover, or once when scrolled into view.`,agent:`For an animated underline or eyebrow rule use <LineDraw trigger="hover|inview">. The line height is --ns-line-h; the timing lives in the styles.css block.`},{name:`ns-underline-elastic`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/primitives/ElasticUnderline.tsx`,value:`ns-underline-elastic · ns-underline-elastic-label · --ns-ul-origin`,note:`A 1px accent rule that grows from the side the pointer entered and retreats toward the side it left; the component writes --ns-ul-origin on enter/leave. The action link beside every <FigureSection> since 2026-09-15. Reduced motion: rule on hover/focus, no transition.`,agent:"For a text link use <ElasticUnderline href> from src/components/primitives/ElasticUnderline.tsx — `inline` inside prose, or standalone with size and icon as a section's action. Never underline a link with a border-b or a transition at the call site."},{name:`ns-glow-halo`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/primitives/GlowButton.tsx · src/components/primitives/DemoButton.tsx`,value:`ns-glow-halo · ns-glow-halo-shell · ns-glow-sheen`,note:`Blurred five-stop halo drifting behind the face while hovered, plus a 700ms sheen sweep and a 2px lift. GlowButton's face is painted by ::after; DemoButton (adopted 2026-09-15) has an opaque face, so its shell takes ns-glow-halo-shell (no ::after) and the sweep is the ns-glow-sheen child. Reduced motion: static glow.`,agent:`The glowing primary CTA is <DemoButton>, which already carries ns-glow-halo. For a plain-faced lit action use <GlowButton>. The halo, sheen and lift all live in the ns-glow-halo block; do not add hover classes at the call site.`},{name:`ns-icon-shift`,path:`src/styles.css · src/components/primitives/IconShiftButton.tsx`,value:`ns-icon-shift · ns-icon-shift-box`,note:`Two stacked glyphs in a clipped box: the first slides out right, the second in from the left (320ms); the gap widens slightly.`,agent:`For an arrow that swaps on hover use <IconShiftButton>; the two-glyph slide is the ns-icon-shift block. Never animate an icon with a transition-* class.`}]}],a=[{title:`Buttons`,description:`Every button is a primitive in components/primitives/ — never an inline className on an <a> or <button>. All three share the sm / md / lg scale and a 44px minimum target (min-h-11).`,entries:[{name:`DemoButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/DemoButton.tsx`,value:`<DemoButton intent="demo" size="md" />`,note:`Primary, filled, and lit: the glass bevel plus GlowButton's halo, lift and sheen on hover (ns-glow-halo, adopted 2026-09-15). Label and href both come from intent (demo · engineer · environment · scoping-call). submit renders a real <button type="submit">.`,agent:`The only primary CTA is <DemoButton />; it already glows on hover, so never wrap it in or place it beside <GlowButton>. Choose the wording with the intent prop (demo, engineer, environment, scoping-call) — never pass a label that disagrees with the href — and the size with size="sm|md|lg". Never pass padding in className.`},{name:`GhostButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/GhostButton.tsx`,value:`<GhostButton label="See the platform" href="/platform/" size="md" icon={ArrowRight} />`,note:'Secondary, outline, with the ns-border-trace hover. Detects external hrefs and sets target/rel itself. With `submit` it renders a <button type="submit"> in the same style — the quiet submit on the newsletter sign-up.',agent:`The secondary action next to a DemoButton is <GhostButton label href size icon />. It sets target and rel for external hrefs itself; do not add them at the call site.`},{name:`IconButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/IconButton.tsx`,value:`<IconButton label="Next slide" icon={ChevronRight} onClick={next} size="sm" tone="quiet" disabled />`,note:"A round icon-only control on button-base: 44px at sm, the icon aria-hidden and `label` the accessible name. A link when href is given. `tone`: quiet (default — hairline, --wash on hover; the CardRail arrows), brand (the ns-brand-fill gradient disc; the chat's send), onBrand (a translucent white disc for use ON a brand fill; the chat header's close).",agent:`A control that is one glyph and one job — a carousel arrow, a close — is <IconButton label icon onClick|href size disabled />. Always pass label (it is the accessible name); never a bare <button> with an SVG.`},{name:`ChatLauncher`,path:`src/components/primitives/ChatLauncher.tsx`,value:`<ChatLauncher ref={launcher} label="Ask NeuralSeek — open the chat" onClick={open} shown expanded busy controls="ns-chat" />`,note:"The site chat's round launcher, fixed bottom-right: a glass disc on a hairline, 56px (64px from sm), with the NeuralSeek mark drawn in particle dots (ParticleMark). Hidden (`shown` false) is visibility: hidden — out of the tab order too. Motion in the chat-mark block. Used once, by SiteChat.",agent:`The chat launcher is mounted once, by <SiteChat> in __root — never place a second one. Its look and motion live in the chat-mark block in src/styles.css.`},{name:`CookieSettingsButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/CookieSettingsButton.tsx`,value:`<CookieSettingsButton />`,note:`The footer's "Cookie settings": a button styled as a footer link that fires ns-consent-open, which reopens <ConsentBanner> so withdrawing is as easy as consenting (RULES.md A16). Rendered by SiteFooter for a FooterLink with action: "cookie-settings".`,agent:`Reopening the cookie banner is <CookieSettingsButton /> (or dispatching CONSENT_OPEN_EVENT from lib/consent.ts); never a second banner or a link to a settings page.`},{name:`ConsentBanner`,path:`src/components/layout/ConsentBanner.tsx`,value:`<ConsentBanner />`,note:`The cookie banner: a card fixed to the bottom (the consent block, ns-consent*), mounted after the intro and only when no choice is stored. Accept cookies (DemoButton) is the primary action; Reject is a muted ElasticUnderline text button beside it, both via their onClick form. Non-modal. While open it lifts the chat launcher above itself. Accept loads GTM through lib/consent.ts, production build only. Mounted once in __root.`,agent:`Anything that needs consent loads through lib/consent.ts after Accept; never add a tag to <head>, and never a second banner. A new tool or category bumps CONSENT_VERSION and changes RULES.md (A16/A18, B1) and the privacy policy in the same commit.`},{name:`WebMcpTools`,path:`src/components/agent/WebMcpTools.tsx`,value:`<WebMcpTools />`,note:`Renders nothing. When the browser exposes WebMCP (document.modelContext, or navigator.modelContext in older builds) it registers two tools for an in-browser agent: ask_neuralseek (the chat's askSeek call, question ≤ 500 characters) and open_contact (navigates to /contact/?intent=…, never fills or submits). Registering sends and stores nothing (RULES.md B10); the tools unregister on unmount. Mounted once in __root.`,agent:`Tools for an in-browser agent go in <WebMcpTools> — one list, mounted once in __root. A tool reuses what the page already does and does nothing until invoked; never one that submits a form or writes storage. The public MCP server (/api/mcp, workers/forms) carries the same ask_neuralseek for agents outside a browser — change both together.`},{name:`PromptButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/PromptButton.tsx`,value:`<PromptButton label="How is NeuralSeek priced?" onClick={() => send(q)} />`,note:`A ready-made question as a chip (rounded-lg): brand hairline, brand-hi text, the --wash tint on hover. Wraps in a row with its siblings (flex flex-wrap gap-2). The chat panel's quick replies.`,agent:`Ready-made questions or choices that act on click are <PromptButton label onClick /> chips in a flex-wrap row; never a bare <button>.`},{name:`ElasticUnderline`,path:`src/components/primitives/ElasticUnderline.tsx`,value:`<ElasticUnderline href="/platform/seek/" size="sm" icon={ArrowRight}>See the console</ElasticUnderline>`,note:"The plain link primitive: a text link with the elastic rule (ns-underline-elastic). Standalone form is on button-base — min-h-11, focus ring, external-href detection, sm/md/lg type scale, optional trailing icon; `inline` is the prose form. The action beside every FigureSection.",agent:`A tertiary text link — the action beside a FigureSection, a link in prose — is <ElasticUnderline href size icon> (inline for prose). It sets target and rel for external hrefs itself; never hand-roll an underlined <a>.`},{name:`CopyButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/CopyButton.tsx`,value:`<CopyButton text="…" label="Copy value" size="sm" />`,note:`A quiet <button> that copies text to the clipboard and reads "Copied" for a moment. Internal tooling only today.`,agent:`For a copy-to-clipboard control use <CopyButton text label size />. It is the third button primitive; do not hand-roll a <button> with its own classes.`},{name:`ToggleButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/ToggleButton.tsx`,value:`<ToggleButton pressed onToggle label pressedLabel size />`,note:`A quiet two-state <button> (aria-pressed) in CopyButton's clothes. Backs <MotionToggle> and the guide's Replay controls.`,agent:`For an on/off control use <ToggleButton pressed onToggle label pressedLabel />. It is the fourth button primitive; never hand-roll a <button> for a toggle.`},{name:`GlowButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/GlowButton.tsx`,value:`<GlowButton label href size icon />`,note:`Blurred drifting halo plus sheen sweep on hover (ns-glow-halo). Its face is painted by the block's ::after, so it carries no bg-* class. Adopted: <DemoButton> carries the same treatment on every page.`,agent:`The primary CTA (<DemoButton>) already has the glow. For a plain-faced lit action use <GlowButton label href size icon />. Do not add a background class to it — the ns-glow-halo block paints the face.`},{name:`IconShiftButton`,path:`src/components/primitives/IconShiftButton.tsx`,value:`<IconShiftButton label href size icon />`,note:`GhostButton's surface without the border trace; the arrow swaps with a slide on hover (ns-icon-shift). Icon defaults to ArrowRight.`,agent:`For a link-style control whose arrow slides on hover use <IconShiftButton label href />. Do not add ns-border-trace to it.`}]},{title:`Media frames`,description:`The one way a product capture is presented. Live demos and the full image catalogue are on the Images page of this guide.`,entries:[{name:`ImageFrame`,path:`src/components/media/ImageFrame.tsx`,value:`<ImageFrame image alt width height frame="glass|browser|none" fade="none|bottom|right" breadcrumb priority tilt featured />`,note:`A <Picture> inside a frame: glass (the screen surface — bevel, top rim, deep shadow), browser (BrowserFrame: the screen with the floating pill strip) or none; optional edge fade so a capture can end softly inside a card. tilt puts the screen on the -12° cascade in a clipped, dot-textured stage; featured adds a brand hairline and violet glow — at most one per section. Static — hover belongs to the containing card.`,agent:`Present a product capture with <ImageFrame image alt width height frame fade breadcrumb />; never a bare <img> or a hand-rolled frame. Use frame="browser" with a breadcrumb for console screenshots and frame="glass" for illustrations.`},{name:`BrowserFrame`,path:`src/components/media/BrowserFrame.tsx`,value:`<BrowserFrame breadcrumb="console › seek">…</BrowserFrame>`,note:`The screen surface (ns-shot-frame) with the browser chrome floating as two pills in its top padding — three dots in a dark pill, a mono breadcrumb pill (ns-canvas-pill) — around any child. Dark in both colour schemes by design. Pass ns-shot-featured in className for the one featured screen.`,agent:`For browser chrome around a screenshot use <BrowserFrame breadcrumb>; prefer <ImageFrame frame="browser"> when the child is a <Picture>.`},{name:`CardMosaic`,path:`src/components/media/CardMosaic.tsx`,value:`<CardMosaic cards layout="grid|strip" columns={3} label="…" />`,note:`Real product card captures (connector nodes, marketplace agents) on the dot-textured stage, as a grid or an ns-marquee strip. Cards are alt=""; the label names the set. No drawing, no logos copied — the cards are photographed from the console.`,agent:`Show a set of small product cards with <CardMosaic cards label>. Never compose a diagram from them, never add a vendor logo file, and mount <MotionToggle /> on any page that uses layout="strip".`}]},{title:`Layout primitives`,entries:[{name:`Section`,path:`src/components/primitives/Section.tsx`,value:`<Section spacing="default" spacingTop="roomy" labelledBy="…">`,note:`The only source of vertical rhythm. spacing: tight · default · roomy · loose · tall; spacingTop overrides the top alone.`,agent:`Vertical rhythm comes only from <Section spacing="tight|default|roomy|loose|tall">, with spacingTop to bias one side. Never write py-*/pt-*/pb-* on a section (RULES.md C4).`},{name:`Container`,path:`src/components/primitives/Container.tsx`,value:`<Container size="default">`,note:`The only source of horizontal gutters and max-width. narrow (max-w-3xl) · default (max-w-5xl) · wide (max-w-7xl).`,agent:`Horizontal gutters and max-width come only from <Container size="narrow|default|wide">. Never write a local max-w-* or px-* in a section (RULES.md C4).`},{name:`SiteHeader / SiteFooter`,path:`src/components/layout/SiteHeader.tsx · SiteFooter.tsx`,value:`<SiteHeader /> … <SiteFooter />`,note:`Every public page renders both around <main id="main">. Links come from content/nav.ts and content/footer.ts.`,agent:`Every public route renders <SiteHeader />, then <main id="main">, then <SiteFooter />. Add navigation links in src/content/nav.ts or src/content/footer.ts, never in the components.`},{name:`Breadcrumbs`,path:`src/components/layout/Breadcrumbs.tsx · src/lib/router-types.ts`,value:`staticData: { crumb: "Sovereignty" }`,note:`Built from useMatches() — every match with a staticData.crumb. A pillar's layout route carries the parent crumb (see routes/trust.tsx).`,agent:`Breadcrumbs are derived from staticData.crumb on each route, never hand-listed. Give a pillar its middle crumb with a layout route like src/routes/trust.tsx, and keep the crumb off the pillar's index.tsx or it renders twice.`}]},{title:`Shape`,entries:[{name:`Radius`,path:`src/components/primitives/DemoButton.tsx · GhostButton.tsx`,value:`rounded-2xl`,note:`Buttons, cards, panels. Inner faces use rounded-xl.`,agent:`Use rounded-2xl for buttons, cards and panels, and rounded-xl for inner faces. Never rounded-full for a CTA.`},{name:`Borders`,path:`src/styles.css (--hairline)`,value:`border border-hairline`,note:`Single border treatment everywhere.`,agent:`Every bordered element uses border border-hairline. Do not use border-white/10, border-border or arbitrary rgba borders.`},{name:`Glass band`,path:`src/components/layout/SiteFooter.tsx`,value:`border-t border-hairline bg-nav-bg/70`,note:`The footer's chrome. Same treatment for any glass band.`,agent:`Use bg-nav-bg/70 with a border-hairline edge for glass section bands, as SiteFooter does.`}]},{title:`Section catalogue`,description:`components/sections/. Each takes its strings as props and owns no copy or page spacing (RULES.md C4). Reuse before writing a new one.`,entries:[{name:`Hero`,path:`src/components/sections/Hero.tsx`,value:`<Hero />`,note:`Homepage opener. The LCP element is text, deliberately.`,agent:`The homepage opener is <Hero>; the accent line uses ns-accent-text. Do not add a second hero component.`},{name:`CloudPartnerBody`,path:`src/components/sections/CloudPartnerBody.tsx`,value:`<CloudPartnerBody p={awsPage} page="aws" path="/aws/" />`,note:`Everything between hero and CTA on /aws/, /azure/ and /ibm-cloud/: buying ActionList → ContainerHub of the cloud's services → CardExplorer of its node cards (or a FigureSection) → the model layer as a HotspotFigure (figure + hotspots from /platform/deployment/) → StoryRail where a named customer runs on that cloud → where ActionList → PlatformConcerns → ComplianceNote → next ActionList. One layout so the three pages cannot drift.`,agent:`For a cloud partner page write a CloudPage content module (content/partners/cloud-page.ts) and render <CloudPartnerBody> between <PageHero> and <Cta />. Never re-lay the sections inline in a route.`},{name:`PageHero`,path:`src/components/sections/PageHero.tsx`,value:`<PageHero titleId eyebrow titleLines titleAccent lede figure actions note />`,note:"`note` (Oct 6 2026) is one quiet line under the buttons — the cloud pages' direct marketplace link. Full-viewport interior opener: headline left, figure right. The accent line is <TextShimmer once> — one light sweep after paint, then at rest.",agent:`An interior page that leads with a figure opens with <PageHero>; one without a figure uses <PageIntro>. Its accent shimmer is inside the section — do not add a second one.`},{name:`PageIntro`,path:`src/components/sections/PageIntro.tsx`,value:`<PageIntro eyebrow titleLines titleAccent? lede actions centered? side? />`,note:"Eyebrow, h1, lede, optional figure beside them. With titleAccent the lines go regular weight and the accent is <TextShimmer once>, as in PageHero. Reading pages carry no figure: `centered` sets the block on the reading column (the article header, eyebrow = topic · N min read); `side` puts a plain panel — a <FactsCard> — beside the heading with no glass wrap (the event page).",agent:"Use <PageIntro> for the top of an interior page that does not need a full-viewport opener. On an article use `centered`; on an event page pass the session's facts as `side={<FactsCard …/>}` — never leave the right half of a left-aligned opener empty."},{name:`PointGrid`,path:`src/components/sections/PointGrid.tsx`,value:`<PointGrid titleId eyebrow title lede items columns action? footnote? widenLast? />`,note:'A titled section over a grid of short title/body cards. Each item is a <SpotlightCard as="li" size="lg"> — cursor-lit rim and wash, still at rest. An item may carry an eyebrow ("Week 1"), points (a short list), an icon (a round .ns-mark glyph above the title, IconName) and an action (one ElasticUnderline at the card\'s foot — the /trust/ documents); the section\'s `action` is one ElasticUnderline under the lede, `footnote` one small line under the grid; `widenLast` lets an odd last card span both columns.',agent:`For a grid of short title/body cards use <PointGrid items columns="two|three">; the items are already SpotlightCards, so do not wrap them in another card.`},{name:`IntroStage`,path:`src/components/layout/IntroStage.tsx · src/lib/intro.ts · .ns-intro*`,value:`<IntroStage /> (in __root, once) + INTRO_BOOT`,note:`The page-load intro: points fly in from every edge of the screen, each on its own delay and spiralling (TrustParticles stagger/swirl), and build the NeuralSeek icon, which lands on the navbar logo's mark as the stage dissolves onto the aurora, started beneath it at the intro's landing phase (≈4.8 s from first paint). Phases: data-intro-phase arriving → landing → leaving (onIntroPhase). Logo only — no other figure. Only on arrival from outside the site; never with reduced motion/data or on automation; any input skips it; ?intro=1 forces it for review.`,agent:`Do not add a second intro or a splash screen. Timing lives in INTRO (lib/intro.ts) and the Intro block in styles.css; who sees it lives in INTRO_BOOT. Test with ?intro=1 — a plain Playwright visit never shows it.`},{name:`FormPage`,path:`src/components/sections/FormPage.tsx`,value:`<FormPage titleId eyebrow titleLines titleAccent lede above below aside form heading? id? />`,note:`The layout every form page shares: headline + optional aside (FormAsideLinks: one or two plain links) on the left, the form card sticky on the right; on a phone heading → form → aside. heading="h2" when it closes a longer page (/internship/).`,agent:`A page whose job is a form uses <FormPage>; put the fields in <FormCard> from components/forms/Fields (Text, Select, Area, Consent, Acknowledge, FormStatus, SentCard) and post through postForm(). Never hand-roll a form column.`},{name:`FigureSection`,path:`src/components/sections/FigureSection.tsx`,value:`<FigureSection titleId eyebrow title lede? body points action caption figure={{ kind: "image" | "video" | "mosaic", … }} side="left|right" split />`,note:'Text beside one real product figure. Renders the figure from data — a browser-framed capture, a <Video>, or a CardMosaic — so the route passes content and a side. Text enters with <Reveal>, the figure with <BlurIn>; the figure never tilts or hovers. `action` renders as an <ElasticUnderline size="sm"> text link with a trailing arrow, not a button.',agent:`For a section that argues one point beside one product figure use <FigureSection> with figure={{ kind, … }} from the content module and alternate side per section down the page. Never wrap a capture in TiltStage below the hero, and never put more than three FigureSections on a page.`},{name:`StepList`,path:`src/components/sections/StepList.tsx · .ns-steps`,value:`<StepList titleId eyebrow title lede steps split actions={<>…buttons</>} footnote />`,note:`An ordered mechanism — a pipeline, a layered defence — as numbered rows with a hairline rail. First and last badges are brand-filled (entry and exit). Numbers are sequencing, never a count. Always one column (two-column mode removed 2026-09-23).`,agent:`For steps that happen in a fixed order use <StepList steps>; for unordered capabilities use <PointGrid>. Do not put numbers in the step titles — the component numbers them.`},{name:`HotspotFigure`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/HotspotFigure.tsx · .ns-hotspot*`,value:`<HotspotFigure titleId eyebrow title lede body figure={{ image, alt, width, height, breadcrumb }} hotspots={[{ id, icon, title, body, x, y, w, h }]} caption side="left|right" />`,note:`A real capture with named regions: scroll down the list beside it and the region lights up in step (focus and click select too; no hover) (a rounded, thinly outlined window; the rest of the capture dimmed; no badge, no purple frame). Every description is always visible; the first region is active at rest. Coordinates are % of the picture. Each row carries an icon (content names it). The overlay is not rendered below md.`,agent:`For a node map or an annotated console screen use <HotspotFigure figure hotspots>: measure x/y/w/h as percentages of the 1600-wide public asset, keep hotspots ≤ ~10 in reading order, give each an icon name from components/icons (the rows carry glyphs, never numbers), and put the descriptions in the content module. Never draw a diagram — annotate a capture.`},{name:`CardExplorer`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/CardExplorer.tsx · .ns-explore*`,value:`<CardExplorer titleId eyebrow title lede label items={[{ id, image, width, height, title, body }]} columns={3} side="left|right" />`,note:`Small real cards on the shot stage (node cards, metric tiles) that describe themselves: the active card lifts and takes the trace-head rim; its title and body crossfade in as plain text under the heading (every item is rendered, stacked, so the block's height never changes; aria-live). The first item is shown at rest.`,agent:`For a set of small captures the visitor should be able to ask about — connector nodes, metric tiles — use <CardExplorer items label>; the panel copy comes from the content module and the cards are decorative (alt=""). Never a tooltip.`},{name:`CardRail`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/CardRail.tsx · .ns-rail*`,value:`<CardRail titleId eyebrow title lede slides={[{ id, image, alt, width, height, breadcrumb?, title?, body? }]} plain />`,note:"A snap-scrolling row of browser-framed captures with previous/next IconButtons and arrow keys on the focusable track. User-driven only — no autoplay, so no pause control. `plain` = unframed small cards (marketplace, nodes) on the shot stage with no captions. Slide width and smooth scroll live in the ns-rail block.",agent:`For three to six captures that tell one story in sequence use <CardRail slides>; it needs no MotionToggle. Do not add a carousel library (RULES.md A1) and do not autoplay it.`},{name:`PhaseRow`,path:`src/components/sections/PhaseRow.tsx · .ns-phase*`,value:`<PhaseRow titleId eyebrow title lede phases={[{ id, title, body }]} actions={<>…buttons</>} footnote />`,note:`Three or four phases read left to right: a connector line through one numbered node per phase, first node at the left edge, middle ones centred, last at the right edge of its card, a LiftCard under each, the last phase lit (brand hairline + wash) as the destination. The line draws and the nodes light once on first view — armed from JS only below the fold; the HTML and reduced motion get the finished row. A column below lg.`,agent:`For a short sequence whose END is the point (scope → build → you own it) use <PhaseRow phases>. A long procedure with detail per step is a <StepList>; parallel options are an <ActionList>.`},{name:`StackFigure`,path:`src/components/media/StackFigure.tsx · .ns-iso*`,value:`<StackFigure stack={{ top: { kind: "chat|inbox|editor|report|channels|slack|image", label, image? }, layers: [label, …, base] }} />`,note:`A deployment as an isometric stack of slabs in SVG (the NatWest Cora illustration, redrawn in tokens): the interface on top with a skeleton of its screen (or a real customer screenshot), the NeuralSeek slab with its wordmark, the named systems, and a brand-gradient base; label pills alternate left/right. Scales as one piece; both themes; slabs part on slide hover, still under reduced motion.`,agent:`Use <StackFigure stack> for a customer story's picture. Every label must come from the story's own text; the top face is skeleton bars, never invented words. Keep labels ≤ ~24 characters — pill widths are estimated.`},{name:`WireFigure`,path:`src/components/media/WireFigure.tsx · wire-symbols.ts · .ns-wire*`,value:`<WireFigure build={wireSymbol("globe").build} label="Itochu: a trade route over the world" />`,note:"The picture on a customer story slide: an abstract form in a few SVG lines, turning slowly, one accent and a pulse moving through; parts on hover. One form per customer, never shared (`wire` in content/customers/showcase.ts). No WebGL, so no context and no still — the first frame is in the prerendered HTML. Holds still under reduced motion and the footer's MotionToggle. All forms: /staging-dev-guide/wire-figures/.",agent:"A customer story's picture is its `wire` key in src/content/customers/showcase.ts, rendered by StoryScene as <WireFigure> from src/components/media/wire-symbols.ts. Give a new story a form no other story uses; a new form is abstract geometry (curves, rings, ruled surfaces, angular layers), never a literal object and never a console capture."},{name:`StoryRail`,path:`src/components/sections/StoryRail.tsx · useRail · .ns-story*`,value:`<StoryRail titleId {...resolveShowcase(page.stories, path)} />`,note:`Named customer stories as wide snap slides: the story's own wire figure (WireFigure, one form per customer) with the flat logo small bottom-left, beside sector, tagline, bullets, deployment and an optional story link. Arrows, arrow keys, dots; no autoplay. Hover lifts the logo; still under reduced motion. <StoryScene story /> is one slide's scene alone (stage + figure + flat logo) — the opener of a /customers/<slug>/ page.`,agent:"To show customers on a page, add `stories: { eyebrow, title, lede, ids }` to its content module and render <StoryRail {...resolveShowcase(...)} />. Stories live once in src/content/customers/showcase.ts; logos come from public/img/logos-color/ (bun scripts/tools/encode-logos-color.mjs) and render flat through .ns-logo. Never put a NeuralSeek console capture on a customer slide — beside their name it reads as their system. No inset cards, pills or colour plates either."},{name:`LogoRow`,path:`src/components/sections/LogoRow.tsx · .ns-logo`,value:`stories: { …, logos: { eyebrow: "Also running NeuralSeek in …", slugs: ["santander", "itau"] } }`,note:`A still, wrapped row of logo-only customers under a StoryRail (the solutions pages' "Also running NeuralSeek in …" line). Same files and sizes as the homepage strip, resolved by slug via logoBySlug() in content/partners.ts; no motion.`,agent:"To list customers that have a logo but no story, add `logos: { eyebrow, slugs }` to the page's `stories` block; StoryRail renders <LogoRow> itself. A slug must exist in marquee.items (content/partners.ts) or the build fails. Never add Children's Health."},{name:`PlatformHeading`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/PlatformHeading.tsx`,value:`<PlatformHeading titleId eyebrow title lede split />`,note:`The eyebrow / title / lede block the platform sections open with. split stagger-reveals the title word by word — used once per page; otherwise the block enters with <Reveal>.`,agent:`Open a platform section with <PlatformHeading>; pass split on exactly one section per page (the first body section) and nowhere else.`},{name:`PlatformPrinciples`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/PlatformPrinciples.tsx`,value:`<PlatformPrinciples titleId eyebrow title lede items />`,note:`Three architectural principles as a row of <LiftCard>s with a numbered mono eyebrow and a glyph assigned by position. The row enters as one block; the heading is the page's split heading.`,agent:`The /platform/ principles row is <PlatformPrinciples items>; glyphs are positional, so reorder copy freely. Do not swap LiftCard for TiltCard — three leaning cards in a row is noise.`},{name:`PlatformLayers`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/PlatformLayers.tsx`,value:`<PlatformLayers titleId eyebrow title lede items footnote />`,note:`The six layers as a uniform 3×2 grid of <LiftCard>s, each with an IconShiftButton into its page and an ImageFrame capture on the -12° cascade; featured on at most one. Never a bento.`,agent:`The six-layer grid is <PlatformLayers items>. Every item needs image/width/height/breadcrumb from content; mark exactly one featured. Keep the grid uniform — uneven spans say one layer matters more.`},{name:`PlatformConcerns`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/PlatformConcerns.tsx · .ns-platform-question`,value:`<PlatformConcerns titleId title lede items />`,note:`Objection and answer, always open: the question is an h3 with a LineDraw underline that draws in view; each row enters with its own <Reveal>. The objections section on every /platform/* page.`,agent:`Close a /platform/* page's argument with <PlatformConcerns items> (question/answer rows, always open — never a <details> accordion here). Other pillars keep <ConcernList>.`},{name:`ConcernList`,path:`src/components/sections/ConcernList.tsx · .ns-faq-row*`,value:`<ConcernList titleId title lede items />`,note:`Objection and answer, stacked; the question is the heading, numbered 01, 02… Hover (.ns-faq-row*): a brand wash sweeps in from the left, the top rule lights left to right, the index turns --glow, the question steps in and the answer comes to full ink. Reduced motion: same end state, no movement.`,agent:`For objection-and-answer content use <ConcernList> — the question is the heading so a skim still reads.`},{name:`ContrastList`,path:`src/components/sections/ContrastList.tsx`,value:`<ContrastList … />`,note:`A comparison one dimension at a time, in prose. Hover (.ns-contrast*): the row lifts onto a brand wash with a rule drawn down its left edge, the heading steps in, the first column quiets and the second comes forward with its label underlined (LineDraw). Used by /compare/*.`,agent:`Comparison pages use <ContrastList> — prose per dimension, never a checkmark table.`},{name:`ActionList`,path:`src/components/sections/ActionList.tsx · .ns-action-card, .ns-mark, .ns-pile`,value:`<ActionList … items={[{ id, title, body, action, mark: { logo: "aws" } | { icon: "shield" } | { wordmark: "levantto" } }]} />`,note:"Cards that each end in one link (action is optional — a card that only describes, e.g. a ContainerHub part, omits it), with a round mark slot: a vendor mark from public/img/marks/ (flat, in the page's ink) or a named glyph — or, for a partner's full logo too wide for the slot, a `wordmark` on its own row above the title (same flat treatment; the /partners/ delivery partners). Four cards or fewer start piled and unstack into a column as the section scrolls (scroll-driven where the browser has it, transform only; a one-shot staggered unstack on enter where it does not, e.g. Firefox; a plain column with reduced motion); five or more are a two-column grid.",agent:"For cards that each end in one link use <ActionList>; give every item a `mark` — `{ logo }` for AWS / Azure / IBM / Kubernetes / Docker / OpenShift, otherwise `{ icon }` from IconName in content/platform/_types.ts. A partner firm's wide logo is `{ wordmark }` (WordmarkLogo in the same file; the SVG in public/img/marks/)."},{name:`FounderProfiles`,path:`src/components/sections/FounderProfiles.tsx · .ns-founder, .ns-founder-photo, .ns-founder-scrim`,value:`<FounderProfiles titleId eyebrow title lede items={[{ id, name, title, body, photo, link }]} footnote />`,note:`The /company/ founders: one card per person, a square-cropped portrait from public/img/people/ running edge to edge and a thin fade at its foot into the card surface, then name, role (in --glow), bio and a LinkedIn GhostButton. Two level, equal-height columns from md, capped at max-w-4xl. Hover lifts the card, draws the rule beside the index and eases the photo in 4%; reduced motion keeps only the colour change.`,agent:`For people (founders, leadership) use <FounderProfiles>; add the portrait to public/img/people/ at 960×1200 and a row to docs/images/people/README.md. Never put a person's photo in an ActionList mark.`},{name:`ContainerHub`,path:`src/components/sections/platform/ContainerHub.tsx · .ns-hub*`,value:`<ContainerHub titleId eyebrow title lede hub items footnote />`,note:'The parts of one container around a central core: ActionList cards in two columns, each joined to the core by a curved line that draws in once on view and lights when its card is hovered or focused. Below lg: the core above a plain list, no lines. Nothing loops. Also the six controls around "Every deployment" on /trust/ (no links, icons from CONTROL_MARKS). `mark="neuralseek"` (the "What runs natively on …" hub of /aws/, /azure/, /ibm-cloud/ via CloudPartnerBody) replaces the core card with the `icon` point figure (TrustHeroFigure, still point-icon) — no card, no visible label.',agent:"To show the components that ship together in one unit, use <ContainerHub> with an ActionSection plus a `hub` label; an even number of items (≤ 6) keeps the columns balanced. Items may omit `action` when the card only describes."},{name:`TrackList`,path:`src/components/sections/TrackList.tsx`,value:`<TrackList … />`,note:`A course or programme track with a status.`,agent:`Programme and course tracks render with <TrackList>.`},{name:`Statement / PullQuote`,path:`src/components/sections/Statement.tsx · PullQuote.tsx`,value:`<Statement titleId title body /> · <PullQuote … />`,note:`A heading and one paragraph; one attributed quote.`,agent:`For a single heading-plus-paragraph beat use <Statement>; for one attributed customer quote use <PullQuote>.`},{name:`Story`,path:`src/components/sections/Story.tsx`,value:`<Story … />`,note:`Customer proof: film left, headline, quote and attribution right.`,agent:`Customer proof with a film uses <Story>, which opens the film in FilmLightbox.`},{name:`Sovereignty / Trust`,path:`src/components/sections/Sovereignty.tsx · Trust.tsx`,value:`<Sovereignty /> · <Trust />`,note:`The homepage chapters with their figures.`,agent:`The sovereignty and trust chapters are <Sovereignty> and <Trust>; their copy is in content/sovereignty.ts and content/trust.ts.`},{name:`ScrollMap`,path:`src/components/sections/ScrollMap.tsx · .ns-scrollmap-panel (+ .ns-hotspot-row, .ns-explore-panel-item)`,value:`<ScrollMap titleId eyebrow title lede panelLabel stages={[{ id, icon, title, body, items: [{ id, icon, title, body }] }]} />`,note:`An ordered list mapped to what handles each entry: stages on the left light as the reader scrolls (useScrollActive, HotspotFigure's rows), and a sticky panel beside them crossfades to the active stage's items — icon, title, description. Focus and click select; no hover. Below lg no panel: each row prints its items as one line. /trust/responsible-ai/ lifecycle.`,agent:`For 'these stages, and what runs at each' use <ScrollMap stages panelLabel>; resolve each stage's items from content the page already renders, never restate them.`},{name:`ComplianceNote`,path:`src/components/sections/ComplianceNote.tsx · .ns-compliance-card`,value:`<ComplianceNote titleId title body heldLabel? builtLabel? builtEyebrow? attested frameworksLede frameworks footnote />`,note:`The compliance statement — deliberately not a badge grid. The attested item on the left (sticky beside cards), the frameworks on the right under their qualifying lede. Frameworks passed as { name, note } are cards: hover or focus one and the attested card crossfades to that framework's note under builtEyebrow, its rim dropping from glow to hairline; leaving fades back (/trust/certifications/). Plain strings stay a row of chips (/sovereignty/).`,agent:`State compliance with <ComplianceNote>, which separates attested certifications from frameworks the product is built to meet. Never render a badge grid. A framework note says what the product contributes inside the customer's environment — never compliant, certified or authorized.`},{name:`PartnerMarquee / PartnerBand`,path:`src/components/sections/PartnerMarquee.tsx · PartnerBand.tsx`,value:`<PartnerMarquee /> · <PartnerBand />`,note:`Continuous logo strip; the reseller signpost on /company/.`,agent:`Logos scroll in <PartnerMarquee>; the reseller signpost is <PartnerBand>. No fictional customers, ever (RULES.md D2).`},{name:`PlatformCarousel / WorkflowCarousel`,path:`src/components/sections/PlatformCarousel.tsx · WorkflowCarousel.tsx`,value:`<PlatformCarousel /> · <WorkflowCarousel />`,note:"The two product carousels. PlatformCarousel's cards stage their capture with <TiltStage>; a card with `targets` (the deployment slide) shows <DeployTargets framed> instead.",agent:`Product captures are shown through PlatformCarousel or WorkflowCarousel; both already carry the video pause control. PlatformCarousel's 3D stage is the TiltStage media component, shared with the /platform/ hero.`},{name:`InviteCard`,path:`src/components/sections/InviteCard.tsx · .ns-conic-ring`,value:`<InviteCard titleId eyebrow? title lede actions />`,note:`One large centred card with the always-on conic rim: eyebrow, h2, one line, buttons. A single invitation set apart from the cards around it (the partner invite on /contact/).`,agent:`For one standalone invitation inside a page, use <InviteCard> with copy from content/ and buttons as actions. The page-closing block is still <Cta />.`},{name:`Cta`,path:`src/components/sections/Cta.tsx`,value:`<Cta />`,note:`Title, one line, two buttons: "Start now" (self-serve signup, filled) and "Request a demo" (outline). Closes every page. Copy in content/cta.ts.`,agent:`Close a page with <Cta>, wrapped in <Reveal>. Its copy and both button targets live in src/content/cta.ts.`},{name:`ContactForm / SupportForm / InternshipForm`,path:`src/components/sections/ContactForm.tsx · SupportForm.tsx · InternshipForm.tsx`,value:`<ContactForm /> …`,note:`The three forms. All POST on submit only, through submitForm (src/lib/form.ts): since Oct 4 2026 staging and production post to the ns-forms Worker on the same origin (/api/forms/*); the Google Apps Script (RULES.md A14) is only the fallback for a build without VITE_FORMS_API.`,agent:`Forms POST through submitForm on submit only, never on load — to the same-origin ns-forms Worker on staging and production. Do not add an external origin, and never test a submit against a production build — it is live there (staging's Worker runs in dry run).`},{name:`ArticleBody / LegalDocument`,path:`src/components/sections/ArticleBody.tsx · LegalDocument.tsx`,value:`<ArticleBody titleId lead? note? sections /> · <LegalDocument doc={…} />`,note:"Long-form renderers; every string arrives in props. ArticleBody sets `lead` (the opening paragraph) larger above the first h2, and from xl shows an 'In this article' list in the left margin, sticky, marking the section being read (useScrollActive).",agent:"Long-form pages render through <ArticleBody> or <LegalDocument doc>; the document itself lives in content/. Put the article's full opening paragraph in `lead` and a 2–3 line standfirst in the header."},{name:`FactsCard`,path:`src/components/cards/FactsCard.tsx`,value:`<FactsCard eyebrow rows={[{ label, value }]} action? />`,note:`A short label/value list on a glass panel with the ns-rim, hairline between rows, optional action under it. The event page's session card (when, length, format, with, recording).`,agent:`When the right half of a reading page's opener needs content, give it facts in a <FactsCard>, not a decorative figure.`},{name:`PricingTiers`,path:`src/components/sections/PricingTiers.tsx`,value:`<PricingTiers id titleId title titleAccent lede tiers heading above buy />`,note:"The /pricing/ tiers, and since Oct 6 2026 the page opener (heading=\"h1\": title with an ns-accent-text tail, breadcrumbs in `above`, a quiet row of marketplace links under the cards in `buy`; no hero above it). One SpotlightCard per tier (wash={false}: rim light only, the Sovereignty card's look) — how it is bought as the eyebrow (never a figure), name, body, note, who/where points, and the tier's button (DemoButton when `cta.primary`, else GhostButton). No conic ring on the featured tier (dropped Oct 6 2026); `stretch` + mt-auto keep the three buttons on one line.",agent:`Pricing tiers render through <PricingTiers>; never put a number in the price slot and never hand-style the tier buttons.`},{name:`AgentCatalogue`,path:`src/components/sections/AgentCatalogue.tsx`,value:`<AgentCatalogue titleId title lede searchLabel allLabel emptyText inputsLabel closeLabel detailsLabel audienceLabel pagerLabel prevLabel nextLabel start fallback />`,note:"The live agent list on /marketplace/ (Oct 6 2026): fetches the same-origin GET /api/marketplace (ns-forms Worker: NeuralSeek's built-in agents from the marketplace instance) after mount; renders `fallback` (the static category PointGrid) while loading, without a Worker, on error or when empty. Search box (inputCls), category chips (ToggleButton), three rows per page (cards per page follow the 1/2/3/4 columns) with a pager of IconButton arrows + ToggleButton page numbers, agent cards in the product's style (category hue from --cat-* via CATEGORY_STYLE: top strip, wash, icon tile, label; green Ready to use pill; 3-line description; SpotlightCard glow via usePointerGlow), a native <dialog> with audience, inputs and Start now.",agent:`To list live catalogue items, use <AgentCatalogue>; never call NeuralSeek from the browser — the Worker holds the key and strips the NTL.`},{name:`MarketplaceLinks`,path:`src/components/sections/MarketplaceLinks.tsx`,value:`<MarketplaceLinks label links className />`,note:`The cloud buy pages (IBM Cloud, Microsoft Azure, AWS Marketplace) as one quiet line with each vendor mark, under Start now: the /pricing/ tiers and the /aws/, /azure/, /ibm-cloud/ heroes (PageHero note, that cloud only). One list, content/cta.ts marketplaces.`,agent:`Direct buy links go through <MarketplaceLinks {...cta.marketplaces} />; never a hand-written list of listing URLs.`},{name:`TrialBand`,path:`src/components/sections/TrialBand.tsx`,value:`<TrialBand titleId eyebrow title titleAccent body label href />`,note:`The /pricing/ self-serve trial as a banner card in the homepage <Sovereignty> card's shape (Oct 6 2026): glass card, ns-glow-edge-track rim leaning toward the cursor (usePointerGlow), a display title with an ns-accent-text tail on the left, the note and a filled DemoButton on the right.`,agent:`For a self-serve trial banner use <TrialBand>; it reuses the Sovereignty card treatment, never a new card style.`},{name:`PlanTable`,path:`src/components/sections/PlanTable.tsx`,value:`<PlanTable titleId eyebrow title lede label columns featured rows={[{ id, label, values }]} />`,note:"Our own plans side by side as a real <table>: string cells, true = tick (labelled Yes), false = dash (hidden No); the featured column tinted with the wash. Narrow screens scroll it inside a labelled, focusable, `relative` region with sticky row labels — the page never scrolls sideways.",agent:`Compare our own tiers with <PlanTable>. Never use it for a competitor — that is <ContrastList>, prose only.`}]}],o=[{title:`What fails the build`,description:`scripts/check-output.mjs reads every prerendered HTML file after vite build. Any one of these exits 1 and the deploy stops.`,entries:[{name:`External origins`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs · RULES.md A1, A14`,value:`https://script.google.com`,note:`Nothing may LOAD from another origin — no fonts, scripts, images, runtimes. Linking (an <a href>) is fine. The single exception is the forms' Apps Script endpoint — since Oct 4 2026 only the fallback of a build without VITE_FORMS_API (the live forms post to the same-origin Worker).`,agent:`Never load anything from a third-party origin — fonts, scripts, WebGL runtimes, images. Self-host it. The only permitted egress is the forms' POST to script.google.com on submit; do not add another (RULES.md A1, A14).`},{name:`Forbidden strings`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs (FORBIDDEN) · RULES.md D2–D6`,value:`const FORBIDDEN = [ … ]  // scripts/check-output.mjs`,note:`Fictional customer names, the old third-party booking link, the previous builder's identity, unverified product counts and placeholder href="#" links must never ship. The list is not reprinted here — quoting it would fail this very page.`,agent:`Before writing copy, read the FORBIDDEN list in scripts/check-output.mjs: no fictional customers, no unverified product counts, no third-party booking link, no mention of the previous site builder, no placeholder href="#". The build fails on any of them (RULES.md D2–D6).`},{name:`One canonical, one h1`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs · src/lib/seo.ts · RULES.md C10`,value:`seo({ path, title, description })`,note:`Only a leaf route calls seo(). A layout route or __root with head() would add a second canonical, and Google ignores both.`,agent:`Build a page's head only by calling seo() from src/lib/seo.ts in the LEAF route's head(). Never call seo() or set a canonical in a layout route or __root.tsx — TanStack does not dedupe links by rel, and two canonicals fail the gate (RULES.md C10). Every page has exactly one <h1>.`},{name:`Copy rules`,path:`scripts/copy-rules.mjs · scripts/check-output.mjs · RULES.md A20`,value:`scanText(visibleText(html), path)`,note:`Banned words, em dashes, UK spelling and the claims we don't make fail the build on every page and in llms.txt, robots.txt and /.well-known/*. All visible text lives in src/content/ (shared labels in src/content/ui.ts).`,agent:`Never write visible text inside a component or route: put it in src/content/ (shared labels in src/content/ui.ts). Load the website-copy and neuralseek-product skills before writing any. The build fails on a banned word, an em dash, UK spelling or a claim we don't make (scripts/copy-rules.mjs, RULES.md A20); change copy-rules.mjs in the same commit as a skill rule.`},{name:`Google keeps the title`,path:`tests/google.spec.ts · src/lib/safe-lazy.ts · RULES.md A19`,value:`safeLazy(() => import("…"))`,note:`Oct 7 2026: one JS file failed in Google's renderer and the homepage was indexed as "Something went wrong". The googlebot project fails a release if any page loses its served title, description or canonical after JS — also with any one JS file blocked.`,agent:`Never use React's lazy() directly — use safeLazy() from src/lib/safe-lazy.ts, which renders nothing if the chunk fails. New always-on components in __root.tsx go inside a <SilentBoundary>. Never let a page's JS remove or change the <title>, description or canonical the server sent; tests/google.spec.ts (project googlebot) blocks the release if it does (RULES.md A19).`},{name:`500 characters of text`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs`,value:`text.length >= 500`,note:`A prerendered page that degraded to an empty SPA shell has no other symptom.`,agent:`Every prerendered page must contain at least 500 characters of visible text — that is how check-output.mjs detects a route whose prerender degraded to an empty shell.`},{name:`Video pause control`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs · RULES.md B6`,value:`data-video-toggle`,note:`A page with a <video> and no pause control fails. <Video> and the carousels already render one.`,agent:`Any page with an autoplaying <video> must render a visible pause control carrying data-video-toggle — use the <Video> component rather than a bare tag (WCAG 2.2.2, RULES.md B6).`},{name:`Contact intents`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs · src/content/contact.ts`,value:`demo · engineer · environment · scoping-call`,note:`A ?intent= slug outside this list compiles and ships, then silently lands on the default heading. The gate is the only thing that catches it.`,agent:`A contact link may only use ?intent=demo, engineer, environment or scoping-call — the keys of CONTACT_INTENTS in src/content/contact.ts. Prefer <DemoButton intent> over a hand-written href.`},{name:`Page floor`,path:`scripts/check-output.mjs`,value:`MIN_PAGES = 30`,note:`A green gate over an empty site is not a pass. The floor exists because CI once shipped zero pages and 231 redirect stubs and reported clean.`,agent:`check-output.mjs requires at least 30 prerendered pages (redirect stubs excluded). If the count drops near it after removing pages, lower the floor deliberately rather than deleting the check.`},{name:`Redirect collisions`,path:`scripts/build-redirects.mjs · src/content/redirects.ts`,value:`✗ redirect "/x" would overwrite the prerendered page`,note:`Stubs are written after the prerender and never over a real page. When a redirected URL becomes a page, remove its entry.`,agent:`When a URL listed in src/content/redirects.ts becomes a real route, remove or repoint its redirect entry in the same commit — build-redirects.mjs exits 1 rather than overwrite a prerendered page.`}]},{title:`Environments`,entries:[{name:`VITE_DEPLOY_ENV`,path:`src/lib/env.ts · .github/workflows/staging.yml · publish-production.yml`,value:`IS_PRODUCTION_BUILD = import.meta.env.VITE_DEPLOY_ENV === "production"`,note:`Set only by the production workflow. Its absence keeps every staging page noindex — and keeps the contact form off the live endpoint. Not import.meta.env.PROD, which is true for any vite build.`,agent:`Tell staging from production with IS_PRODUCTION_BUILD from src/lib/env.ts (VITE_DEPLOY_ENV === "production"), never with import.meta.env.PROD. On a production build pages are indexable and the contact form posts to the live endpoint — never submit a form against one to test it.`},{name:`Internal routes`,path:`src/content/internal-routes.ts · vite.config.ts · scripts/build-redirects.mjs · RULES.md D7`,value:`INTERNAL_ROUTES = ["/staging-dev-guide"]`,note:`This guide. Built on staging; on a production build the prerender filter drops it and its redirect stub (→ /) is written instead.`,agent:`A route that must exist on staging only goes in INTERNAL_ROUTES (src/content/internal-routes.ts): vite.config.ts drops it from a production prerender and build-redirects.mjs skips its stub on other builds. Give it seo({ index: false }) as well (RULES.md D7).`},{name:`Prerendered $slug pages`,path:`vite.config.ts · RULES.md A4`,value:"pages: [...comparisons.map((c) => ({ path: `/compare/${c.slug}`, prerender: { enabled: true } }))]",note:`Dynamic routes are not auto-discovered. The pages list in vite.config.ts is derived from the content modules so it cannot drift.`,agent:"A new $slug route only builds if its paths are added to `pages` in vite.config.ts, derived from the content module the route renders. Never rely on crawlLinks alone (RULES.md A4)."}]},{title:`Conventions the gates do not catch`,entries:[{name:`Dependency direction`,path:`RULES.md C1`,value:`routes → sections → primitives`,note:`A primitive never imports a section; a section never imports from routes/.`,agent:`Keep imports pointing one way: routes import sections, sections import primitives, and never the reverse (RULES.md C1).`},{name:`No arbitrary values, no hardcoded colours`,path:`RULES.md C3`,value:`bg-surface, not bg-[#2A2638]`,note:`Use the token utility. The few arbitrary values that exist are documented one-offs.`,agent:`Do not write arbitrary Tailwind values (bg-[#…], text-[…px]) or hardcoded colours; use the semantic token utilities from styles.css (RULES.md C3).`},{name:`Route file length`,path:`RULES.md C5`,value:`≤ ~80 lines`,note:`A longer route file means a section is missing.`,agent:`Keep a route file under roughly 80 lines — head(), content imports and a list of sections. If it grows past that, extract a section (RULES.md C5).`},{name:`Client-only code`,path:`RULES.md C7, C8`,value:`useEffect + lazy(() => import(…))`,note:`Anything touching window, canvas or WebGL runs after mount. One ambient GL context; a second only while its section is on screen.`,agent:`Anything that touches window, canvas or WebGL runs after mount (useEffect) and is lazy-imported so it stays out of the initial chunk. Never hold two WebGL contexts at rest (RULES.md C7, C8).`},{name:`Motion pairs`,path:`RULES.md C12 · CLAUDE.md`,value:`component/hook + one ns-* block in styles.css`,note:`Never a transition-*, animate-* or @keyframes at a call site. The inventory is on the Figures & motion page.`,agent:`A new motion effect is a component next to Reveal.tsx (or a hook next to use-in-view.ts) plus one named ns-* block in src/styles.css with its own reduced-motion override — never a transition or keyframe at a call site. Reuse the inventory first (RULES.md C12).`},{name:`Commit messages`,path:`CLAUDE.md`,value:`Do not mention Claude, AI or any assistant in commit messages.`,note:`Repo convention.`,agent:`Never mention Claude, AI or any assistant in a commit message for this repository.`}]}];export{t as a,e as i,a as n,o,i as r,r as s,n as t};